WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.460
In July of 1776, King George III still towered over New York City.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.060
Not in person, in lead.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.280
At Bowling Green, near the tip of Manhattan,

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:17.540
stood a gilded 4,000-pound statue of King George on horseback,

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:19.340
dressed like a Roman emperor.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:24.940
It had been erected just six years earlier as a monument to British power.

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:31.820
But by the summer of 1776, Americans no longer saw majesty in this statue.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:33.920
They saw tyranny.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:45.680
On July 9, 1776, George Washington gathered his troops in New York and had the neatly adopted Declaration of Independence read aloud.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:56.980
The words had barely settled before soldiers, sailors, and patriots surged down Broadway to Bowling Green and toppled the King George statue.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:06.560
While the crowd saw tyranny toppled, Connecticut patriot Oliver Wolcott saw bullets.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:14.940
4,000 pounds of lead bullets for a Continental Army Wolcott knew would be desperate for ammunition.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:22.500
So Walcott had the shattered pieces of King George loaded onto boats and shipped to Connecticut.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:33.480
From there, ox carts hauled the remains of King George more than 60 miles over rough country roads to Walcott's home in Litchfield.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:39.900
And in the Walcott family orchard, a shed became a foundry.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:48.780
His wife Laura, his daughter Mary Ann, and local neighbors worked over melting pots pouring the king's lead into bullet molds.

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:52.840
42,088 musket balls in all.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:58.140
Some of those bullets were fired two years later at the Battle of Monmouth,

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:06.940
the brutal New Jersey showdown that Washington's army proved could stand in the open field against British regulars.

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:09.640
You have to love the irony.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:13.380
Britain sent America a monument to empire.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.600
America sent it right back at muzzle velocity.

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:22.240
So let's celebrate our nation's 250th birthday

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.700
as we remember patriots like Oliver Wilcott

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:27.360
who made it all possible.

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:30.480
From the White House, I'm Peter Navarro.
